1
00:00:23,926 --> 00:00:29,632
Hello and welcome to the NPTEL MOOC's course on the Foundation for Virtual and Augmented Reality Systems.

2
00:00:30,733 --> 00:00:40,222
We are going to start a new topic today: Interactive System Development Life Cycle.

3
00:00:40,242 --> 00:00:49,251
If we reflect on our earlier learning, let us quickly see what we have learned so far.

4
00:00:49,271 --> 00:00:53,595
Now, earlier at the very beginning, we talked about

5
00:00:54,790 --> 00:01:05,141
AR/VR systems and subsequently we talked about the relationship of these extended reality systems to interactive systems.

6
00:01:05,623 --> 00:01:14,353
So, we talked about the idea of interactive systems first and then learned the relationship between XR systems and interactive systems.

7
00:01:14,413 --> 00:01:24,004
Essentially what we have learned is that XR systems are nothing but a class of interactive systems because eventually the users of the

8
00:01:24,946 --> 00:01:27,189
XR systems are supposed to be laypersons.

9
00:01:29,271 --> 00:01:40,560
Also, we noted how design and development of interactive systems are affected by various human factors and

10
00:01:40,560 --> 00:01:51,038
in the context of that knowledge, we also talked about human factors and their relationship to XR systems.

11
00:01:54,562 --> 00:01:58,646
Eventually, that discussion led to the concept of user-centric design.

12
00:01:58,766 --> 00:02:11,279
So, when we are talking about building an interactive system, essentially what we try to do is build and design a system that has followed a user-centric design approach.

13
00:02:11,299 --> 00:02:12,700
Now, what is user-centric design?

14
00:02:12,740 --> 00:02:23,691
That is essentially to involve the users in the design process so that we can understand their needs, their expectations, and their goals.

15
00:02:24,397 --> 00:02:29,523
And accordingly, we can design the system so that all those are supported.

16
00:02:30,223 --> 00:02:36,490
If we do not do that, then it will lead to systems that are unacceptable to the users.

17
00:02:37,792 --> 00:02:40,855
In this context, we also talked about the usability of a system.

18
00:02:41,536 --> 00:02:52,007
And we mentioned that the goal of any interactive system is to make the system usable with respect to a specific group of users in a specified context of use.

19
00:02:55,160 --> 00:02:57,202
So that is what we have learned so far.

20
00:02:57,222 --> 00:03:07,011
Note that when we talk of usability, we talk of a specific group of users, a specified context of use, and specific tasks.

21
00:03:07,031 --> 00:03:14,459
So it is not that we try to make a system which will be usable for any user in any context for any task.

22
00:03:14,479 --> 00:03:16,140
So these things are important.

23
00:03:16,180 --> 00:03:20,625
Now the question is, how do we develop such a system?

24
00:03:20,705 --> 00:03:22,146
How do we take into account

25
00:03:22,717 --> 00:03:25,420
the users in the overall development process?

26
00:03:25,841 --> 00:03:31,567
So, that is what we are going to learn in this and subsequent few lectures.

27
00:03:35,212 --> 00:03:51,711
So, the basic thing in the user-centered design approach is to, or in other words, the objective of user-centered design is to design products that increase usability.

28
00:03:52,535 --> 00:03:59,805
Now, in our context, products are nothing but the application software that we are dealing with.

29
00:04:03,951 --> 00:04:15,686
Now, we have seen that when we use the term user-centered design or user-centric design or human-centric design or participatory design,

30
00:04:15,686 --> 00:04:21,916
so there are different terms, different phrases to represent the same concept.

31
00:04:22,268 --> 00:04:39,015
The basic thing there is that such approaches, in whatever name we call it, such approaches essentially emphasize either active or passive involvement of,

32
00:04:39,015 --> 00:04:51,297
the users' involvement of the users in the overall design lifecycle which includes design and implementation.

33
00:04:51,800 --> 00:04:55,924
So, the goal is to involve the users either actively or passively.

34
00:04:59,208 --> 00:05:15,285
The core design concern when we are talking about user-centric software is designing usable systems to cater to the needs and expectations of layman users or laypersons.

35
00:05:15,766 --> 00:05:19,630
So, essentially we are trying to build

36
00:05:20,318 --> 00:05:27,607
application software, which are XR systems, which of course depends on some hardware, but that is a separate consideration.

37
00:05:28,809 --> 00:05:33,056
Now, this application software is supposed to be interactive software,

38
00:05:33,056 --> 00:05:41,505
interactive system, because it is supposed to be used by those who may not know how the hardware works, how the software works.

39
00:05:41,525 --> 00:05:43,868
They may not need to know.

40
00:05:43,888 --> 00:05:45,009
So, they are the laypersons.

41
00:05:47,032 --> 00:05:49,735
For them, we need to go for

42
00:05:50,137 --> 00:05:59,030
user-centered design approach or user-centric design approach or human-centric design approach or participatory design approach or

43
00:05:59,030 --> 00:06:13,834
similar such phrases we can use, but all point to a particular approach where we try to include or involve the end users in the

44
00:06:13,834 --> 00:06:19,857
design process either actively or passively with the end goal of having

45
00:06:20,765 --> 00:06:24,457
usable systems having our application software usable.

46
00:06:24,457 --> 00:06:39,103
Now in order to achieve that goal, in order to achieve the goal of having a usable product, usable software application software, certainly we cannot work in a haphazard manner.

47
00:06:39,103 --> 00:06:49,185
We cannot work in an ad hoc manner; we require a systematic approach, a proper step-by-step process we should follow so that at the end

48
00:06:49,857 --> 00:06:58,692
the process itself ensures that we get as output of the process the usable application software.

49
00:07:02,939 --> 00:07:07,246
Now, this brings us to the idea of software development lifecycle.

50
00:07:08,247 --> 00:07:12,895
Probably the majority of you, if not all, are already aware of these things; probably you have

51
00:07:14,714 --> 00:07:18,199
studied this in a software engineering course.

52
00:07:18,359 --> 00:07:22,866
If you have gone through any such course, then you know what a software development life cycle is.

53
00:07:23,327 --> 00:07:32,589
The idea here is that we follow such development life cycles to develop and deploy software so as to

54
00:07:32,589 --> 00:07:41,093
comprehensively capture and represent the design and development activities that should go into building the software.

55
00:07:43,267 --> 00:07:49,854
So, essentially such life cycles give us a very systematic, structured way of looking at the whole development process.

56
00:07:54,559 --> 00:08:08,554
Now, if we can use a single term to represent the idea of software development life cycle, we can use the term software engineering, engineering a software.

57
00:08:08,574 --> 00:08:12,818
So, when we say we are trying to engineer a software, we need to follow a software development

58
00:08:15,278 --> 00:08:19,946
Or in other words, we need to build the software in some stages.

59
00:08:20,387 --> 00:08:28,509
Now, these stages essentially capture or structure the whole process and through their connection,

60
00:08:28,509 --> 00:08:41,741
they essentially let us represent in a very structured manner how the development process should take place so that at the end we get what we want to get.

61
00:08:43,139 --> 00:08:55,236
In our context, we want to get a usable software, and we should have a structured software development lifecycle that leads us to a usable software.

62
00:08:55,256 --> 00:09:01,304
Now, let us try to understand what is the problem, why we need to follow a development lifecycle.

63
00:09:03,527 --> 00:09:11,458
So, when we are trying to engineer a software towards achieving our goal, we said that we need to follow a

64
00:09:12,620 --> 00:09:17,265
a development life cycle as a structured way of achieving our goal.

65
00:09:17,865 --> 00:09:25,613
Now, to understand the complexity of the problem and why we need a structured way, let us see a simple, very simple example.

66
00:09:25,633 --> 00:09:29,617
That is, let us try to create a very simple calendar application.

67
00:09:31,038 --> 00:09:32,780
We are all familiar with such applications.

68
00:09:32,860 --> 00:09:37,124
It comes in our smartphone as a ready-made app.

69
00:09:37,465 --> 00:09:40,888
But if we are asked to do this, what should we do?

70
00:09:42,438 --> 00:09:44,640
How are we going to approach the problem?

71
00:09:48,244 --> 00:09:50,507
So, we can come up with various ideas.

72
00:09:52,529 --> 00:10:06,264
The first thing that comes to our mind when somebody tells us to build a calendar application, a calendar app, is to come up with a design idea, how it should, how the interface should look.

73
00:10:06,285 --> 00:10:08,547
So, one such possible design idea can be

74
00:10:09,404 --> 00:10:13,891
create a grid-like structure typically found on a physical calendar.

75
00:10:14,312 --> 00:10:18,359
So, we have experience dealing with physical calendars.

76
00:10:21,424 --> 00:10:27,614
Because of that experience, that knowledge, we immediately start thinking of something similar in the digital version of the calendar.

77
00:10:30,719 --> 00:10:36,508
And this grid-like structure will have headings on each cell of the grid,

78
00:10:37,298 --> 00:10:44,987
which represents the name of the month like in a physical calendar.

79
00:10:45,007 --> 00:10:56,460
Then we can create sub grids in each cell to hold the dates like again in a physical calendar and render the entire structure on the screen.

80
00:10:56,481 --> 00:10:57,722
So that is one design idea.

81
00:11:02,327 --> 00:11:07,153
Also, additionally, we may like to add some features which typically are

82
00:11:07,589 --> 00:11:15,782
activities associated with calendars such as highlighting a date and time for certain occasions, events as reminders.

83
00:11:16,002 --> 00:11:21,657
So, we can highlight the current date and day, current month or

84
00:11:21,657 --> 00:11:33,209
we can also highlight some specific dates where we set some reminders for certain activities, some events, some meetings, something like that.

85
00:11:33,229 --> 00:11:35,993
So, if we put this into practice,

86
00:11:36,867 --> 00:11:38,629
This may look something like this.

87
00:11:40,071 --> 00:11:42,013
So, this is our device.

88
00:11:43,755 --> 00:11:49,862
We are assuming that we are making the design for a smartphone or mobile device.

89
00:11:50,703 --> 00:11:51,504
This is our device.

90
00:11:52,125 --> 00:11:53,627
Then we have grid-like structures.

91
00:11:55,489 --> 00:12:04,900
So, there are cells in the grid and each cell is further divided into cells.

92
00:12:06,837 --> 00:12:17,868
So, each cell is subdivided into a 6 by 6 subgrid to hold maximum 31 dates as we know in the English calendar and we can highlight the current date.

93
00:12:18,269 --> 00:12:21,452
So, we require a 3 by 4 grid structure.

94
00:12:22,994 --> 00:12:25,676
Each cell holds the dates for a month.

95
00:12:26,237 --> 00:12:29,701
So, each cell represents a month and inside each cell,

96
00:12:29,781 --> 00:12:33,925
there are days like shown here in this figure.

97
00:12:35,390 --> 00:12:40,579
So, the outer grid structure should be 3 by 4 so that it can hold 12 months.

98
00:12:41,861 --> 00:12:51,777
The inner grid structure should be at least 6 by 6 so that 31 days can be shown.

99
00:12:55,724 --> 00:12:56,505
Should we stop here?

100
00:12:57,607 --> 00:12:58,208
Should we

101
00:12:58,981 --> 00:13:05,370
Assume that whatever we have designed is the only possible design or the best design or the most usable design.

102
00:13:07,753 --> 00:13:09,796
Can I draw a conclusion based on this design?

103
00:13:11,238 --> 00:13:19,750
These are some questions that come to our mind when we are trying to implement such a design idea.

104
00:13:21,813 --> 00:13:24,277
But there can be many more such designs, in fact.

105
00:13:25,258 --> 00:13:26,019
This is only one.

106
00:13:26,340 --> 00:13:27,942
So there can be another design where

107
00:13:30,892 --> 00:13:35,816
each screen displays the dates of one month.

108
00:13:35,877 --> 00:13:48,708
So, at a time only one month is shown on a screen; say, for example, January is shown here and under January there are dates of January shown in this form of a grid.

109
00:13:51,791 --> 00:13:58,317
Now, if I want to see the next month or the previous month, we need to swipe next or

110
00:13:59,748 --> 00:14:05,815
either swipe left, right, or up, down depending on our design choice.

111
00:14:08,038 --> 00:14:17,590
Here also we can highlight the current date in a month view and screen changes happen through tap or swipe.

112
00:14:18,311 --> 00:14:27,662
So, any sort of interaction we can deploy to go to the next month or the previous month or even the previous year.

113
00:14:32,131 --> 00:14:33,332
That can be another design.

114
00:14:34,433 --> 00:14:35,374
Now, which one is better?

115
00:14:36,315 --> 00:14:49,328
If I ask you this simple question: whether the previous one is better or this one is better, or which one is more usable, the previous one or this one, can we immediately answer that question?

116
00:14:49,348 --> 00:14:52,892
In fact, there can be many such possibilities.

117
00:14:53,873 --> 00:14:58,998
It is not that only these two are possible designs.

118
00:15:00,559 --> 00:15:01,260
There can be

119
00:15:02,067 --> 00:15:04,770
in fact, a very large number of such possible designs.

120
00:15:06,591 --> 00:15:12,117
And the challenge is how to choose which one is the right one.

121
00:15:12,457 --> 00:15:19,844
Now, here in our context, the right one means which one is the best in terms of usability.

122
00:15:22,527 --> 00:15:24,809
Now, what would be the size of the design space?

123
00:15:25,690 --> 00:15:29,153
Design space means the set of all possible designs.

124
00:15:29,173 --> 00:15:31,095
So, suppose there are n elements,

125
00:15:33,573 --> 00:15:35,856
or n objects to be placed on a screen.

126
00:15:36,416 --> 00:15:42,964
So, we can actually arrange these objects in n factorial ways on a screen.

127
00:15:42,984 --> 00:15:52,035
So, that is the typical size of a design space when we have n distinct objects and none of these objects are repeated.

128
00:15:53,176 --> 00:16:01,926
If we allow repetition, then certainly the number will increase manifold.

129
00:16:04,792 --> 00:16:20,719
And for a suppose calendar type thing where if we go by the first view, then you can try to figure out what is n and what is the total size of the design space.

130
00:16:21,440 --> 00:16:28,312
Vis-a-vis, if we go to the next view again, you can find out what is n and what is the size of the design space.

131
00:16:28,873 --> 00:16:30,175
Now the thing is that

132
00:16:33,783 --> 00:16:39,568
In general, for a calendar app, we have a fixed N, that is the number of things to be displayed.

133
00:16:40,389 --> 00:16:51,118
So, those are month name, then date, possibly year, and the N is very large.

134
00:16:51,298 --> 00:16:55,042
So, correspondingly, we can have many, many such designs.

135
00:16:56,023 --> 00:17:00,887
In fact, a very large number of designs which are unmanageable or even unthinkable.

136
00:17:01,387 --> 00:17:02,288
Now, the thing is,

137
00:17:03,550 --> 00:17:12,359
Clearly, some of these designs, some of all these possible designs in the design space are invalid because dates have to be ordered in a particular way.

138
00:17:12,460 --> 00:17:17,025
It is not possible to arrange all dates in random ways.

139
00:17:17,546 --> 00:17:30,520
These are some things that come through our experience, knowledge, intuition that if the theoretical design space is n factorial,

140
00:17:31,546 --> 00:17:40,039
The majority of such design choices may be invalid because of practical considerations and we are left with few choices.

141
00:17:40,600 --> 00:17:53,840
However, rarely these remaining choices are very small or only one choice is left.

142
00:17:54,861 --> 00:18:00,169
In most cases, we can have a reasonably significant number of

143
00:18:00,588 --> 00:18:03,511
choices if we are dealing with practical systems.

144
00:18:05,153 --> 00:18:18,247
So, although we can immediately invalidate many designs based on our experience and intuition, but still we may be left with 10, 15, 20 choices which seem to be reasonable.

145
00:18:19,308 --> 00:18:25,414
And the challenge is to identify the best among all these possible design choices.

146
00:18:31,047 --> 00:18:35,872
Now, to identify the best, we cannot rely on our intuition.

147
00:18:38,214 --> 00:18:52,668
For very experienced developers, for very experienced designers, probably that is true, but not for everyone in general, that our intuition may not help us lead to a single best solution.

148
00:18:53,149 --> 00:18:59,535
So, we need to follow a systematic approach and that systematic approach is provided by the

149
00:19:00,089 --> 00:19:03,415
software development life cycles or SDLC.

150
00:19:03,455 --> 00:19:18,341
So, essentially the role of SDLC is to help us identify the best among all possible design, all possible valid design choices following a systematic approach.

151
00:19:22,749 --> 00:19:24,392
Now, those who have already done

152
00:19:24,912 --> 00:19:30,718
a course on software engineering are well aware of different software development life cycles.

153
00:19:31,699 --> 00:19:33,681
The classical model is the waterfall model.

154
00:19:33,721 --> 00:19:40,509
Of course, this is not used in practice, but it is primarily used for pedagogical purposes.

155
00:19:40,529 --> 00:19:45,794
We have spiral model, evolutionary model, iterative model and so on.

156
00:19:49,218 --> 00:19:54,784
I will not go into the details of any of these models because those are not part of this course.

157
00:19:56,266 --> 00:20:07,644
I am sure that the majority of you have already gone through them or if not, you can consult any book on software engineering to get more ideas on software development lifecycle.

158
00:20:07,664 --> 00:20:21,666
What we are more interested in here is the interactive systems and the corresponding development lifecycle, software development lifecycle that helps us build usable interactive systems.

159
00:20:23,755 --> 00:20:27,241
So, we talked about interactive systems which should be usable.

160
00:20:27,962 --> 00:20:34,493
Now, it requires the users to be taken into account in every stage of the design.

161
00:20:34,533 --> 00:20:39,802
So, we need to involve the users in our design process as active or passive participants.

162
00:20:41,785 --> 00:20:48,396
Now, that is not an easy thing to include users' needs and users' expectations in our design process.

163
00:20:49,861 --> 00:20:52,143
It requires iteration between stages.

164
00:20:52,203 --> 00:21:18,889
So, the software development lifecycle is nothing but a set of stages to be followed in a particular sequence.

165
00:21:24,083 --> 00:21:28,207
In a particular sequence, ideally these things should be followed.

166
00:21:28,587 --> 00:21:32,126
Now, if we are bringing users into the picture,

167
00:21:32,126 --> 00:21:43,081
Then these stages, the sequence in which it has to be followed gets altered, gets changed significantly and there appears lots of iteration between stages.

168
00:21:43,282 --> 00:21:45,904
So, it is kind of a loop.

169
00:21:45,924 --> 00:21:51,930
We complete a stage, go to the next stage, then we may need to go back to the previous stage rather than proceeding to the

170
00:21:52,332 --> 00:21:54,174
next stage in the sequence.

171
00:21:54,194 --> 00:22:02,064
So, that looping happens more frequently when we are dealing with interactive systems and usable systems.

172
00:22:05,608 --> 00:22:19,926
So, in order to implement a user-centric design approach for building interactive software in general and extended reality software in particular, we require a highly iterative lifecycle.

173
00:22:20,311 --> 00:22:22,613
A highly iterative software development lifecycle.

174
00:22:24,575 --> 00:22:33,263
And these iterations between stages, the looping between stages, are there to account for the user feedback.

175
00:22:33,823 --> 00:22:41,830
Although I am using the term feedback here, it essentially means involving the users in different ways, not necessarily through feedback only.

176
00:22:48,817 --> 00:22:50,318
So, such an iterative

177
00:22:51,749 --> 00:22:58,435
Software development lifecycle for interactive system development may look something like this.

178
00:22:58,835 --> 00:23:01,137
As you can see, we have several stages.

179
00:23:02,098 --> 00:23:06,662
Feasibility study, then requirement gathering, analysis, and specification.

180
00:23:08,944 --> 00:23:18,952
Then there is this design prototype evaluate cycle, which comprises the design stage, prototyping stage, and early evaluation stage.

181
00:23:19,453 --> 00:23:20,794
Then we have code design.

182
00:23:22,597 --> 00:23:33,056
Coding and implementation, then code testing, empirical study or usability study, and finally deployment and maintenance.

183
00:23:35,420 --> 00:23:42,293
And there are iterations, say for example, from this design prototype evaluate cycle to the requirement gathering stage.

184
00:23:43,657 --> 00:23:49,724
Then within the design prototype evaluate cycle, there are iterations as shown here between these three stages.

185
00:23:50,605 --> 00:23:56,352
Then we have iteration here, we have iteration here.

186
00:23:58,775 --> 00:24:04,021
So, it is a pretty good amount of iteration we can see.

187
00:24:04,962 --> 00:24:12,010
So, it comprises several stages and several iterations between the stages, and we need to follow such

188
00:24:12,462 --> 00:24:17,909
iterative development lifecycle in order to build usable interactive systems.

189
00:24:17,929 --> 00:24:22,615
So, let us quickly have a look at what these stages are and what they do.

190
00:24:25,818 --> 00:24:29,523
So, let us start with the requirement gathering stage.

191
00:24:29,743 --> 00:24:40,056
We are excluding the feasibility study stage as this is basically whatever is done here is

192
00:24:40,289 --> 00:24:42,773
true for any software, not only interactive systems.

193
00:24:42,793 --> 00:24:59,901
So this is a common stage where we check for the feasibility of the project in terms of available resources, available money, available time, and so on.

194
00:25:02,007 --> 00:25:10,795
More importantly, we should focus on, we will be focusing on the stages where there is a difference between any software and interactive software.

195
00:25:10,835 --> 00:25:12,897
So, we start with this requirement gathering stage.

196
00:25:13,718 --> 00:25:29,533
Now, this stage from this very stage itself, we should involve the end users along with the customer or client of our system, which is typically the practice in any software development.

197
00:25:30,627 --> 00:25:38,117
Typically when we say requirement analysis, we refer to the requirements of the client.

198
00:25:38,137 --> 00:25:45,226
Now in the case of interactive systems, along with client requirements, we need to take into account the requirements of the end users.

199
00:25:45,547 --> 00:25:50,273
So from this stage onwards, we need to involve the users.

200
00:25:51,232 --> 00:25:59,520
There are several methods to gather end user requirements such as contextual inquiry, ethnographic studies, cultural probes, and so on.

201
00:25:59,981 --> 00:26:06,508
So, these are part of this stage.

202
00:26:06,528 --> 00:26:11,393
Then we have the design prototype evaluate loop as shown here.

203
00:26:13,214 --> 00:26:18,640
This entire loop is the design prototype evaluate loop.

204
00:26:19,649 --> 00:26:25,717
This loop is required for the refinement of early designs based on user feedback.

205
00:26:26,278 --> 00:26:28,001
So, we have involved the users.

206
00:26:28,201 --> 00:26:36,372
So, whatever feedback they give, based on that, we can modify the design and we need to do it in several iterations.

207
00:26:37,033 --> 00:26:42,160
So, suppose based on this requirement analysis, we came up with a design idea like our calendar app.

208
00:26:42,180 --> 00:26:47,648
Then we can quickly create a prototype of this idea and get it quickly evaluated.

209
00:26:49,585 --> 00:26:52,228
Then we identify problems through evaluation.

210
00:26:52,609 --> 00:27:03,000
Now, based on those problems we refine the design, then again evaluate, then again prototype, evaluate and this process continues till we reach a

211
00:27:03,000 --> 00:27:10,691
stage where not many significantly different problems are being identified through this evaluation.

212
00:27:10,711 --> 00:27:15,036
So, in that case, we can say that our design has stabilized and we can move to the next stage.

213
00:27:17,530 --> 00:27:20,654
So this cycle involves many iterations.

214
00:27:20,674 --> 00:27:31,807
So it is essential that each of these stages can be completed quickly so that many iterations happen within a reasonable time.

215
00:27:31,827 --> 00:27:33,288
So it should be performed quickly.

216
00:27:33,308 --> 00:27:39,456
The whole cycle should be less time-consuming.

217
00:27:39,596 --> 00:27:43,200
Otherwise, the overall turnaround time will increase significantly.

218
00:27:46,859 --> 00:27:59,095
Now, this design-prototype-evaluate loop includes interface design and interaction design, these two activities.

219
00:27:59,836 --> 00:28:09,990
So, when we say that we are trying to design the system, essentially there are two parts.

220
00:28:10,010 --> 00:28:15,617
We are designing interface and interaction when we talk of design here in this design-prototype-evaluate cycle.

221
00:28:18,263 --> 00:28:30,842
Later on, there is another design part that is code design, which is separate from the design that we have mentioned in the design-prototype-evaluate cycle.

222
00:28:31,162 --> 00:28:39,134
So the design-prototype-evaluate cycle primarily deals with design of the interface and interaction from the user's point of view based on user requirements.

223
00:28:41,738 --> 00:28:44,322
Then when it comes to coding and code testing,

224
00:28:45,145 --> 00:28:52,352
It can be done in the traditional way as is typically done in any software engineering process.

225
00:28:56,436 --> 00:29:03,283
Of course, here we have code design, then coding and implementation, then code testing.

226
00:29:04,344 --> 00:29:08,369
And through testing, if we find some issues, then we can go back to our code design part.

227
00:29:08,429 --> 00:29:09,870
So there is one iteration here.

228
00:29:11,285 --> 00:29:22,799
However, the fewer these number of iterations, the better because code design is a very highly skilled process that requires skilled manpower and is costly.

229
00:29:23,680 --> 00:29:26,704
So, it is better to identify problems.

230
00:29:27,745 --> 00:29:33,312
It is better to properly design at this stage so that later on problems are fewer.

231
00:29:35,315 --> 00:29:38,178
Otherwise, it is very costly to rewrite codes and test.

232
00:29:39,560 --> 00:29:41,022
So, that we have to keep in mind.

233
00:29:42,976 --> 00:29:46,321
Then comes the empirical study part.

234
00:29:46,341 --> 00:29:53,191
This is another important stage which differentiates any interactive system from that of any other software system.

235
00:29:54,192 --> 00:29:58,638
So, this stage is required to ensure that the product conforms to usability.

236
00:29:58,698 --> 00:30:00,341
Note that here we have one.

237
00:30:01,522 --> 00:30:07,791
So, at three places in this iterative software development life cycle, we have evaluation.

238
00:30:07,871 --> 00:30:10,435
One is early evaluation that is

239
00:30:10,567 --> 00:30:21,317
As which is part of this design prototype evaluate cycle, then we have code testing or evaluation of the code which is part of coding and implementation, and thirdly, empirical study.

240
00:30:22,678 --> 00:30:31,907
Now, this early evaluation is kind of evaluating the ideas of interface and interaction design quickly with fewer resources and less time.

241
00:30:33,609 --> 00:30:38,093
Code testing tests the executability of the code.

242
00:30:38,714 --> 00:30:43,801
That means through this testing, we ensure that there are no bugs, and the code executes properly.

243
00:30:43,921 --> 00:30:52,273
However, this testing, code testing, does not tell us whether the system is going to be usable.

244
00:30:52,974 --> 00:31:03,529
It tells us that the system is executable, the code is executable, bug-free, but it does not tell us whether it is usable from the end user's point of view.

245
00:31:04,330 --> 00:31:07,354
That happens when we perform empirical studies.

246
00:31:08,633 --> 00:31:15,301
So, the empirical study stage is required to ensure the product conforms to usability.

247
00:31:15,321 --> 00:31:24,352
Now, this involves systematic testing with the end users, which is totally different from how we test our code.

248
00:31:25,053 --> 00:31:34,884
Code testing is a different activity, and empirical study or usability testing with end users is a different activity.

249
00:31:35,625 --> 00:31:37,948
And this empirical study is unique.

250
00:31:39,312 --> 00:31:41,996
to the interactive system development.

251
00:31:42,016 --> 00:31:51,350
It is typically not part of any other software development where end users are not involved.

252
00:31:51,370 --> 00:32:07,834
So, the key considerations in this overall iterative design is that the loops that are there, other than the design prototype evaluate cycle, should not be frequent.

253
00:32:08,270 --> 00:32:10,273
So, we have loops at several places.

254
00:32:10,313 --> 00:32:18,464
For example, between this design prototype evaluate cycle and the requirement gathering.

255
00:32:18,544 --> 00:32:21,067
So, we have this loop.

256
00:32:21,087 --> 00:32:27,316
Then we have a loop between code design and code testing as shown here.

257
00:32:28,477 --> 00:32:35,627
We also have a loop between design prototype evaluate cycle and

258
00:32:38,358 --> 00:32:52,705
code coding and implementation and empirical studies so this loop and also we have loop within this design prototype evaluate cycle as

259
00:32:52,705 --> 00:33:06,120
shown here at several places we have loop now this only this loop design prototype evaluate early evaluate only this loop

260
00:33:07,670 --> 00:33:12,878
is designed in a way such that they require less time, and can be performed quickly.

261
00:33:13,839 --> 00:33:26,244
Other loops, particularly the loop between code design, coding and code testing or the loop between design prototype evaluate cycle,

262
00:33:26,244 --> 00:33:35,972
through coding and empirical study, these loops if they happen frequently

263
00:33:36,830 --> 00:33:43,076
then that will have a significant bearing on the overall outcome of the project, the turnaround time of the project.

264
00:33:44,217 --> 00:33:46,659
It may lead to abandonment of the project as well.

265
00:33:46,719 --> 00:34:00,693
So, we should be very careful while completing the stages in such cases so that the requirement to go back to previous stages does not even arise.

266
00:34:01,534 --> 00:34:02,955
Otherwise, it will lead to

267
00:34:04,083 --> 00:34:12,293
escalation in development cost in terms of manpower, time, resources, and even may lead to abandonment of the project.

268
00:34:14,396 --> 00:34:22,867
So that is the overall idea of what we should do to build interactive systems which are usable.

269
00:34:23,648 --> 00:34:29,094
And in our case, we are talking of extended reality systems which are usable.

270
00:34:29,614 --> 00:34:35,163
So we have learned the idea of ISDLC, that is interactive system development lifecycle.

271
00:34:35,824 --> 00:34:42,038
We have seen the stages of the lifecycle broadly comprising of requirement analysis stage,

272
00:34:42,038 --> 00:34:52,027
design prototype evaluate loop, then coding and implementation stage and empirical study or empirical testing stage.

273
00:34:56,344 --> 00:35:04,374
Earlier we talked about interactive systems, what they are, what usability is, and what the need is for having a usable system.

274
00:35:05,095 --> 00:35:11,536
So, when you learn this lecture side by side or go through this discussion side by side,

275
00:35:11,536 --> 00:35:22,875
you will get a clearer picture of interactive systems and how to develop those with respect to our basic concern that is extended reality systems.

276
00:35:24,357 --> 00:35:34,197
In subsequent few lectures, what we are going to do is to focus on the requirement gathering interface interaction design and

277
00:35:34,197 --> 00:35:47,897
evaluation aspects of interactive system development because as we have mentioned the feasibility study or the code testing phase are anyway standard phases.

278
00:35:48,137 --> 00:35:53,942
So, there is not much difference between how good regular software is built

279
00:35:54,985 --> 00:35:58,028
following these stages and how interactive software is built.

280
00:35:58,448 --> 00:36:04,794
The difference is in the requirement gathering stage, design prototype evaluate stage, and empirical study stage.

281
00:36:04,814 --> 00:36:07,163
So, in the next few lectures,

282
00:36:07,163 --> 00:36:21,550
we are going to devote some time to a discussion of these stages, and we are going to use a case study to understand these stages with respect to our extended reality systems.

283
00:36:27,065 --> 00:36:34,292
In the immediately next lecture, we are going to introduce the case study, whatever example we are going to talk about.

284
00:36:35,813 --> 00:36:44,511
We are going to introduce that idea and then in subsequent lectures, we will explain each of these stages in terms of that case study and how

285
00:36:44,511 --> 00:36:56,312
we have taken care of these concerns through our design effort with respect to that case study we are going to discuss so that you get a hands-on

286
00:36:56,883 --> 00:37:02,839
of a practical system and the theoretical concepts.

287
00:37:02,859 --> 00:37:11,382
So, to understand these topics better, you can refer to this book, Human-Computer Interaction, User-Centric Computing for Design.

288
00:37:13,218 --> 00:37:24,270
Here the interactive system development life cycles, the stages are discussed in more detail with respect to broader interactive system development concerns.

289
00:37:25,952 --> 00:37:31,498
So, if you are interested to know more, please refer to this book.

290
00:37:31,518 --> 00:37:33,581
So, with that, we have come to the end of this lecture.

291
00:37:33,901 --> 00:37:41,149
I hope you could follow the concepts and I am looking forward to meeting you all in the next lecture.

292
00:37:41,568 --> 00:37:44,811
Until then, thank you, goodbye, and happy learning.

